    "data": {
      "A": "Research exact, output-order-preserving search pruning. Check identity first, then use sound invariants derived from Boolean adjacency: loop flags, degree multisets and consistent joint color refinement. Do not use raw input edge counts, because duplicate edges collapse. Replace flat enumeration with equivalent recursive Heap blocks; reject blocks when their fixed suffix assignments violate invariants or adjacency constraints. Precompute each skipped block's exact terminal permutation so subsequent traversal remains unchanged. Preserve original vertex labels, every nonidentity matching permutation and their Heap order; never discard automorphism-equivalent answers. Prioritize inexpensive pruning before elaborate refinement.",
      "B": "Research a faster verifier while retaining the original Heap traversal. Cache a failing adjacency pair: a swap not touching either endpoint cannot repair that failure, so verification can be skipped. When affected, recheck the witness and rescan only if necessary. After a known-valid permutation, only pairs incident to the swapped positions can become invalid. Compare this certificate approach against exact incremental mismatch counting and the original early-exit scan; O(n) maintenance on every swap may lose to early rejection. Evaluate flat-byte or portable packed adjacency to remove vector<bool> proxy overhead. Include diagonal entries and preserve all output behavior.",
      "C": "Research low-overhead input and output, using the unchanged traversal as a clean control. Disable unnecessary iostream synchronization and tying, replace per-solution endl flushing with bounded buffered serialization, and preserve trailing spaces, newlines and the exact negative message. Avoid collecting all solutions in memory. Measure full-program improvements rather than assuming parsing or flushing dominates. Four-core execution is optional only if supported by the unchanged build and justified by measured workload: use coarse Heap blocks, deterministic ordered output and a serial path for small jobs. Do not introduce unconditional thread overhead into this roughly 8 ms baseline.",
      "G": "Integrate incrementally from the best validated child, measuring every combination against its standalone inputs through the unchanged harness. First consider compatible representation and buffering improvements, then structural pruning and verifier state. Skipped Heap blocks must correctly update or invalidate cached witnesses, mismatch counts and permutation metadata. Audit size mismatch, identity early return, loops, duplicate edges, nonisomorphic graphs and multiple nonidentity mappings. Require the reference's complete output sequence, not merely a valid isomorphism. Keep compiler, tests, oracle, budgets and target unchanged. Retain the fastest validated standalone program whenever integration regresses; gains are not presumed additive.",
      "analysis": "Only Problem A and src/brute_isomorphism.cpp are relevant. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.008142 seconds on the ARM64 four-core target; no workload sizes or profiling are supplied. Factorial enumeration, verification and flushing are plausible costs, not measured bottlenecks. Crucially, identity succeeds immediately, but otherwise the source prints every matching permutation in Heap order, including trailing spaces. Returning the first nonidentity solution or changing enumeration order would alter reference output. Lower-triangle comparison includes self-loops, and repeated input edges collapse into Boolean adjacency. Research should balance algorithmic pruning against low-overhead improvements without specializing to the four observed cases."
    },
